libssh2 for OpenVMS
===================

This is libssh2 @VERSION@, the client-side SSH2 library, built natively for
OpenVMS @ARCH@ with VSI C against VSI's SSL3 kit (OpenSSL 3.0), from the
unmodified release plus the patches in the vms-libssh2 project:

    https://github.com/issinoho/vms-libssh2

Kit version: @KITVERSION@.  libssh2 is free software under the BSD-3-Clause
licence; see COPYING.  RELEASE-NOTES is upstream's for this release.

What is installed
-----------------
    LIBSSH2$ROOT:[LIB]LIBSSH2.OLB     the object library, for static linking
    LIBSSH2$ROOT:[LIB]LIBSSH2.OPT     link options: the library and SSL3's libcrypto
    LIBSSH2$ROOT:[INCLUDE]LIBSSH2.H, LIBSSH2_SFTP.H, LIBSSH2_PUBLICKEY.H
    LIBSSH2$ROOT:[DOC]                this file, COPYING, RELEASE-NOTES
    SYS$STARTUP:LIBSSH2$STARTUP.COM   defines LIBSSH2$ROOT

There is no shareable image and there are no commands: a program links the
library into its own image, so it needs only SSL3 where it runs, not this kit.

Setting up
----------
The kit installs LIBSSH2$STARTUP.COM into SYS$STARTUP and runs it once.  To
define LIBSSH2$ROOT at every boot, the system manager adds this line to
SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:

    $ @SYS$STARTUP:LIBSSH2$STARTUP.COM

Building a program with it
--------------------------
    $ define/process OPENSSL SSL3$INCLUDE:
    $ cc /names=(as_is,shortened) /pointer_size=32 -
         /define=(_USE_STD_STAT=1,_LARGEFILE) -
         /include=LIBSSH2$ROOT:[INCLUDE] prog.c
    $ link prog,LIBSSH2$ROOT:[LIB]LIBSSH2/options

The library is compiled so, and a program must match it:
  - /NAMES=(AS_IS,SHORTENED): libssh2's names are mixed case, and some are
    longer than 31 characters.
  - /POINTER_SIZE=32 (or none): it uses SSL3's 32-bit libcrypto.
  - _USE_STD_STAT and _LARGEFILE: libssh2_scp_recv2 fills a struct stat, and
    without them a program's struct stat is smaller than libssh2's.
SSL3 must be installed (SSL3$INCLUDE, SYS$SHARE:SSL3$LIBCRYPTO_SHR32).

Not built: zlib compression (libssh2 offers none to the server), and the
WinCNG, mbedTLS, wolfSSL and libgcrypt back ends.
